piper-tts-server for Debian/Ubuntu noble
========================================

Dependency on piper-tts
-----------------------
This package depends on the Debian package piper-tts (>= 1.8.0), which
provides the piper Python library, the /usr/bin/piper command and its
dependencies (python3-onnxruntime, python3-numpy and python3-pathvalidate).
No pip command is run and nothing is downloaded during installation.

piper-tts must be available in the configured repository or installed
previously. Removing piper-tts-server does not remove piper-tts, because it
is an independent package and may be used by other software.

This package also depends on speech-dispatcher, which provides the daemon
and the sd_generic module used by the integration. It depends on
pulseaudio-utils for paplay, on speech-dispatcher-espeak-ng for the
espeak-ng fallback, and on systemd for the socket/service units and
journalctl.

A running PulseAudio or PipeWire sound server is still required for audio
output; the package only installs the client tools.

Voices
------
Voices are installed in /usr/share/piper-voices by the independent packages
piper-tts-voices-{ar,ca,de,en,es,fr,it,ru} (piper-tts-voices repository, one
branch per language, versioned independently). This package recommends them:

    sudo apt install piper-tts-server                        # all
    sudo apt install --no-install-recommends piper-tts-server  # none
    sudo apt install piper-tts-server piper-tts-voices-ca piper-tts-voices-es  # some

If those packages are not available in the configured repositories, apt
skips them without error and the .onnx and .onnx.json files can be copied
manually to /usr/share/piper-voices (PIPER_VOICES_DIR in the systemd unit).

After installing or updating a voice, restart the server to discover it
(or wait for it to exit after 900 s without use):

    sudo systemctl restart piper-tts.service

Configuration
-------------
The server is a system unit (piper-tts.service, activated by
piper-tts.socket at /run/piper.sock). The unit sets all variables; they can
be changed with a drop-in:

    sudo systemctl edit piper-tts.service

Environment variables set in the unit:
- PIPER_SOCKET (/run/piper.sock): server Unix socket.
- PIPER_VOICES_DIR (/usr/share/piper-voices): voices directory.
- PIPER_IDLE_SECONDS (900): seconds without use before the server exits.
- PIPER_MAX_LANGUAGES (2): maximum voices in the LRU cache.
- PIPER_DEFAULT_LANG (auto): default language; auto derives it from the
  system locale (LANG/LC_*). A specific code (ca, es, ...) is also accepted.
- PIPER_SENTENCE_SILENCE (0.2): silence between sentences, in seconds.
- PIPER_LOG_LEVEL (INFO): log level.

The service runs as a systemd dynamic user (DynamicUser=yes) with
NoNewPrivileges=yes; it only reads voices and accepts connections through
the socket.

Voice selection
---------------
speech-dispatcher allows selecting the voice by name; the package forwards
it to the server (voice parameter) with precedence over the language:

    spd-say -y thorsten "Hallo"   # German (thorsten)
    spd-say -y pau "Hola"         # Catalan (alias for upc_pau)
    spd-say -L                    # list available voices

Names: kareem (ar), pau/upc_pau (ca), thorsten (de), lessac (en),
sharvard (es), siwis (fr), paola (it), denis (ru). If the requested voice
does not exist, the language is used; if that does not match either, the
client falls back to espeak-ng (with a warning on stderr). Applications
that set SET_SYNTHESIS_VOICE (Firefox, etc.) work the same way. Direct
equivalent:

    piper-tts-client --voice lessac --lang en

Messages and translations
-------------------------
Source messages are in English. The client, piper-tts-test and the server
command-line help use gettext, with catalogs installed in
/usr/share/locale/{es,ca}/LC_MESSAGES/piper-tts-server.mo. The service
journal remains in English so it is stable for debugging.

The locale is taken from LANG/LC_*; for example:

    LANG=es_ES.UTF-8 piper-tts-test --help
    LANG=ca_ES@valencia piper-tts-test --help
    LANG=C piper-tts-test --help

To show the piper-tts.service journal while testing, use -d/--debug:

    piper-tts-test -d
    piper-tts-test -d -v thorsten

The journal is shown with timestamped lines. Without -d, no journal output
is produced.

Backups and uninstallation
--------------------------
Before modifying the configuration, the package saves .bkp copies:

- /etc/speech-dispatcher/speechd.conf.bkp: copy from before the first patch.
- /etc/speech-dispatcher/modules/piper_sd.conf.bkp: copy of the previous file
  if it already existed on first installation.

Removing (dpkg -r) or purging (dpkg -P) the package restores those copies
and leaves the configuration as it was before installing piper-tts-server.
Note: restoring speechd.conf also reverts changes made after installation.

Automatic integration
---------------------
- dh_installsystemd enables piper-tts.socket (system unit) and starts it;
  the service is activated on demand. On each boot only the socket is
  enabled.
- postinst adds AddModule/DefaultModule for piper to
  /etc/speech-dispatcher/speechd.conf (backup in speechd.conf.bkp) and
  enables the espeak-ng module.
- NOTE: a user file ~/.config/speech-dispatcher/speechd.conf takes
  precedence over /etc; if it exists, adjust or remove it so the package
  configuration applies. The package only manages the system configuration.

Commands
--------
- /usr/bin/piper-tts-server: server (socket at /run/piper.sock).
- /usr/bin/piper-tts-client: client (reads text from stdin, plays through
  paplay; --voice option; libespeak-ng fallback if the server does not
  respond).
- /usr/bin/piper-tts-test: tests the installation by playing a phrase with
  each installed voice (language, all; -v NAME for a specific voice; -d to
  show the server journal; warns if no voice is available):

      piper-tts-test              # all installed voices
      piper-tts-test ca           # one voice
      piper-tts-test es "Hola"    # custom text
      piper-tts-test -v thorsten  # one specific voice
      piper-tts-test -d           # all voices plus the journal
